st that we have to  have much more expanded and available  testing we then have to follow that up  with the contact tracing that can help  essentially drop a net over new cases  when we find that  and if we have these in place along with  the search capacity we need in hospitals  that when fake cases finally start to  dip down then we can safely start to  reopen with cautions we will still need  to keep distance from each other we'll  still need to ensure that we're wearing  masks in public but we leak police have  a fighting chance at preventing a second  surge with those guidelines in mind how  do governors and mayors balance the  known health effects of unemployment and  loneliness with the known health effects  of the virus and is pandemic yes so this  is a this is an unprecedented situation  that we're in and and I appreciate that  there are really tough choices to make  here but one thing we have to avoid is  the false choice between people's health  and the economy if we really want to get  the economy back up and running if you  want people to to not lose any more than  they've already lost we've got to make  sure that we are taking care of people's  health and that we're making it safe if  we don't do that if we open prematurely  then what happens is we will have  another surge and everyone will have to  retreat back in to stay at home measures  which will actually hit the economy even  harder later on but as much as you know  people talk about the economic impact  which is serious and the direct health  impact which is real there's another  impact that we haven't talked about as  much and that's the impact on our social  lives as I wouldn't worry about it it's  the social recession that we may incur  as a result of the prolonged physical  distancing and fear of the virus that  may deepen our loneliness over time and  that has real consequences for our  health as well you know one way to  mitigate that social recession as you  call it is to ramp up testing 